[quote][b]I'm working on all of these mods on my own.[/b] If you'd like to help, extend or fix one of them, co-author a new one, or join one of my bigger projects, I'm very open to it. Send me a message here on Nexus or on the [b]FarCry:Modding Discord Server[/b].[/quote]

[size=6][b]JackAll[/b][/size]

Far Cry 2 keeps everything inside 13 packed archives. That's around 215,000 files, and the game finds them by a hash of the filename instead of the name itself. The only archive a mod is allowed to touch is patch.dat, so installing a mod means rebuilding that archive.

JackAll opens all of it. It shows every archive as one big file tree, decodes the files into something you can actually read and edit, and packs your changes back into a patch.dat that the normal, unmodified game loads. No DLL, no injection, nothing running inside the game process.

The name is for the Jackal, and for "jack of all trades". One tool for the whole job instead of six converters and a batch file, which is what we had before.

[line]

[size=5][b]What it can do[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Mods[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579414-242500304.png[/img]

This is your load order. Mods apply from top to bottom, and if two of them change the same file the lower one wins. Every mod has a checkbox, so you can turn one off without deleting it. A mod here is just a folder or a zip with game paths inside it (worlds\world1\generated\...), which is exactly what you get when you unpack an archive, so old community mods work as they are. If a mod is the old kind that ships a full replacement patch.dat and patch.fat, and most FC2 mods are, use [b]Import legacy mod[/b]: it compares that mod to a clean game and keeps only the files it really changed.

[b]Deploy mods[/b] builds everything into the game, [b]Revert to original[/b] puts the clean archive back. You can't wreck your install by clicking too much. JackAll saves your original patch.dat once as patch.dat.vanilla, and every build starts from that backup again, never from the file that happens to be there. So building twice gives you the exact same bytes, turning a mod off really removes it, and a failed build leaves the game untouched. If two mods change different parts of the same file they get merged instead of one of them winning silently: the entity libraries, the animation rules, menus and HUD, AI brains, controls, game mode settings, particle effects and fonts all split into small pieces that land side by side. If two mods set the same value differently, setting it in your workspace decides it. [b]Check for dead edits[/b] finds edits that a later entity library overrides, which is the kind of change that looks fine in the tool and does nothing in game. A mod can also carry a plugins\ folder, and that one goes to bin\plugins\ for FCSE instead of into the archive.

[size=4][b]Files[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579405-386245121.png[/img]

All 13 archives merged into one tree, resolved in the same order the game resolves them. Over 150,000 files, and anything a mod changed is highlighted, so you can see what you actually touched instead of guessing. Click a file to preview it, export it, replace it or revert it. Replacing a file with the wrong kind of file, say a .wav where a sound bank belongs, asks first. A modded file can be diffed against the original, and [b]Export original[/b] always gets you the clean version back. The filter box matches any word, and you can narrow it with ext:, arch: and hash:.

More than 50,000 files in the game have no known name, only a hash. You can still mod those. JackAll guesses the type from the first bytes, puts them under _unknown\, and saves your edit as _hash\<crc32>.<ext>, which the builder writes back at exactly that hash. Every file also gets a references panel: what points at this file, and what this file points at, both clickable. For a file nobody can decode, that is usually the only real information you get about it.

[size=4][b]The value editor[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579405-1120529920.png[/img]

Almost everything people want to mod (weapons, AI, prices, vehicles, patrols) sits in .fcb files. JackAll decodes them into a normal property tree with real field names and types, so you edit values and not hex, and not XML either. Big files like an entity library are split into one piece per entity, so opening a weapon opens that weapon instead of a huge document. Nothing is written until you press save.

When you save, only that one entity piece lands in your workspace, as a small file. That is the thing that lets two mods live together, and it's explained under [b]How to use[/b] below. The same editor also opens the PersistenceDB inside a savegame, because a save is the same kind of file.

[size=4][b]Textures, audio and models[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579400-392500134.png[/img]

Most file types get a real viewer instead of a hex dump. .xbt textures show a preview and split into a .dds plus a small XML header, and you can build that pair back into a working .xbt. An .xbm material opens as an editor: every property its shader takes, grouped the way the game's own shader descriptors group them, with colour swatches and texture thumbnails, and a warning under anything the game would quietly ignore, like a misspelt property or a texture that isn't there. [b]New material[/b] makes one at a new path. .xbg models get a 3D preview where you pick the LOD and the damage state, and RealTree species draw as actual trees. .sbao audio plays right there, exports as ogg or mp3, and imports basically any audio file you have: ffmpeg comes with the app and converts it to the 48 kHz stereo ogg the engine wants.

.spk banks open as a tree of what plays what: random picks, switches and layers down to each sample, which you can play, export and replace. The game uses two codecs in there (ogg vorbis and IMA ADPCM) and JackAll figures out which one it's looking at. Every retail bank also says what it is, taken from where the game uses it: "weapon · reload, 1st person (ak47)", or the speaker and the subtitle for speech. Type spk:"ak47 reload" in the filter and you have the AK-47's reload sounds. Everything you change here goes into your workspace first. Nothing reaches the game until you press Deploy.

[size=4][b]Model export for Blender[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579401-168886117.png[/img]

[b]Export as .fc2model[/b] on any .xbg collects the model, its materials, its textures, the skeleton next to it and, if you want, every animation bank that uses it. All of that goes into one file with zero Dunia formats inside: JSON and plain float arrays for the mesh, JSON for the materials, PNG for the textures. [b]Apply .fc2model[/b] takes the file back from your 3D editor and stages the changed game files into your workspace, and it shows you the list of files it will touch before it does anything.

The split is on purpose. JackAll knows the byte layouts, and the Blender add-on knows what a scene should look like. The add-on has no format code in it at all. In practice this means the art half of a custom weapon is one file and one plugin, instead of twenty small scripts.

The Blender side is a separate download here on Nexus: [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/farcry2/mods/371]Model editor (Blender plugin)[/url] ([url=https://github.com/jbebe/farcry-sdk/tree/main/tools/BlenderFC2]source[/url]). It imports a .fc2model pack, gives you the mesh, the materials and the rig as a normal Blender scene, and writes your edit back into the pack for [b]Apply .fc2model[/b] to stage.

[size=4][b]Map[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579409-961283114.png[/img]

The Map tab loads a world, lets you fly around it in 3D, and edit it. On the side you get the list of layers a FC2 map is built from, and you can toggle each one. The terrain draws with its real textures and its baked lighting, plus water, roads, rivers, footpaths, the drawn zone outlines, and the vegetation of every sector with each plant as its own model. Placed objects draw as real models or as markers, you can filter them by mission layer, search them, and click them either in the list or in the viewport.

Everything without a model gets its own symbol so you can still find it: lights in their own color, trigger boxes as wireframe, AI cover and guard spots, door and window hints for the AI, particle and sound emitters, and the navmesh the AI walks on, as its triangles and the links between them. It draws with Direct3D 11, so it runs on any Windows 10 PC: on your graphics card, or on Windows' own software renderer where the driver can't, as over remote desktop. A line under the viewport shows what each frame costs.

Select anything and move or rotate it with the gizmo or by typing the numbers, copy and paste it (into the other world too), delete it, or drag a new one in from the archetype library. The inspector edits an object's fields and components, and there are handles for trigger zones and lights, event links between objects, prefabs, mission layers, and undo and redo all the way back. [b]Check[/b] lists the mistakes that save fine and fail silently in game, like a soldier standing off the navmesh or an object dragged out of the sector that loads it. [b]Save[/b] writes each moved or added object as its own small file and each deletion as one line, so two mods editing different objects in the same sector merge. I haven't loaded a map edit in game yet, so test yours before you share it.

[b]New map[/b] starts a world of your own: the game's flat template under your name, one sector or 8 × 8 (512 m). You edit it like any other world, raise ridges and lay out grass and trees in two small plan files, and [b]jackall-cli world pack[/b] builds everything the game needs from it: the sectors, the entity library, the sector list, the terrain, the vegetation, and a navmesh over the ground and everything standing on it, so soldiers can walk and take cover there. Load any save and travel there from the console. Roads and rivers stay as the template has them, and like map edits, nothing made this way has been played yet.

[size=4][b]Archetypes[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579409-1583776340.png[/img]

Every object placed in the world is a small difference on top of an archetype, and archetypes are declared in several libraries that override each other in a fixed order. The Archetypes tab resolves that for you, so the definition you edit is the one the game really reads. If a later library overrides it, you see straight away that it's dead instead of losing an hour on it. The whole override chain is listed next to the archetype and you can open each layer on its own, along with which of your mods edit it. Editing opens the normal value editor on the piece where the winning definition lives.

[size=4][b]AI[/b][/size]

The AI tab collects the numbers that decide how soldiers see, shoot and behave. Soldier archetypes with their spotting, vision cones, marksmanship, reaction time and toughness, each next to the base game's value and what it does. How soldiers fire each weapon, down to how many shots they miss after hitting you on each difficulty. The curves those settings name, drawn as charts. The odds of optional tactics, like grabbing a car to chase you or shooting the barrels next to you, at every stage of the campaign. And the brains themselves, as a tree of plans and tasks with their parameters, where a parameter the game never reads is struck through. Saving a brain recompiles it and stages only the plans and tasks you changed, so two AI mods can change the same brain and both work.

[size=4][b]Animations[/b][/size]

Which animation a character plays is decided by one big rule graph: for every situation (reloading, aiming, sprinting, climbing into a car) a list of rules saying "in these conditions, play this clip, like this". The Animations tab shows it as tables. Pick a weapon, find the situation, and change which clip a rule plays, how fast, whether it can be interrupted, or the conditions it plays under. Every rule shows whether it can ever win and what else plays through it, so you know what a change reaches. [b]Copy weapon set[/b] gives a new weapon index every rule of an existing gun, which is where a new weapon's animations start. Each situation saves as its own piece, so mods changing different situations merge, and the .mab player shows the clips themselves on stick figures.

[size=4][b]Saves[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579416-73000027.png[/img]

The Saves tab finds your .sav files and shows the in game screenshot, the world, your character name, the save time, the act, how much of the game is complete, the difficulty, the DLC it uses and how many objects it stores. The PersistenceDB inside opens in the same value editor as everything else and writes straight back into the .sav, so a value that got stuck is fixable without starting the campaign over.

It's also here to explain something that has confused FC2 modders for about fifteen years: a save stores values that overlap with almost every entity library, and an .fcb mod cannot override what the save already has. That's why a data mod usually needs a new game. Being able to see what your save is holding beats guessing.

[size=4][b]Menus and mission logic[/b][/size]

[img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/1471/images/370/370-1787579412-855183503.png[/img]

.mgb files are the game's own UI format. JackAll decodes one into a full tree with a property grid, so every widget, action, state and color is reachable. The "Add" list only offers the classes the engine itself accepts in that spot, so adding an element or declaring a new class is a normal edit instead of an impossible one. This is what makes a custom options page inside the game possible at all. A package merges area by area across mods, so two mods adding to the HUD both keep theirs.

[b]Make a font[/b], on the Mods tab, draws a font the game can use from any TrueType or OpenType file. Pick the face, the size and a variable font's weight, see sample text set the way the game sets it, and it's staged so any UI text can name it.

Domino is the mission scripting system, and it ships as generated Lua. JackAll rebuilds a script from domino\user\ into the box and wire graph it was made in, lays it out for you, takes the pin names from the node scripts and the original names from the *.debug.lua file next to it. It's read only for now: you can see what a mission does, you just can't change it here yet.

[size=4][b]Collision[/b][/size]

.hkx files are Havok collision: what you bump into, stand on and shoot. [b]Open in HKX Editor[/b] draws an object's collision over its model, lists the bodies and shapes as a tree, and lets you type over any value or drag a shape to move, turn or resize it. Saving keeps Havok's own bookkeeping in step, the MOPP tree and the bounds included, so the game sees the new shape instead of the old one. A reshaped weapon or prop no longer has to keep the donor's collision.

[line]

[size=5][b]Format support[/b][/size]

Everything below is JackAll's own code, one implementation per format, tested against the files the game actually ships.

[list]
[*][b].fat / .dat[/b] - the archive pair, LZO compressed, indexed by hash. Reads every entry, rebuilds patch.dat/patch.fat. Every shipped .fat comes back byte for byte identical[/*]
[*][b].fcb[/b] - binary object trees: entities, weapons, AI, world sectors. Decodes and encodes, splits into one piece per entity, property tree editor, merges edits coming from different mods[/*]
[*][b].rml[/b] - binary XML, used for manifests and the translated text table. Decodes and encodes, text editor with a diff against the original[/*]
[*][b].mgb (+ .mgb.desc)[/b] - Magma UI packages: menus and HUD. Full decode and encode plus a real editor, and a verify pass that checks a package only uses names it declares[/*]
[*][b].xbt (+ .dds)[/b] - textures, block compressed, with a streaming header. Splits and rebuilds, decodes BCn to pixels for previews and model packs, encodes back with the same compression[/*]
[*][b].xbm[/b] - materials: shader, texture slots, parameters. Edited property by property against the shader's own list, made new, and written back through a model pack[/*]
[*][b].xbg (+ .xbgmip)[/b] - models, with LODs, damage states and skinning. Decodes and encodes, 3D preview, .obj export, editable through a model pack[/*]
[*][b].skeleton[/b] - skeletons. Reads and writes, byte identical on every shipped file[/*]
[*][b].mab[/b] - animation banks. Reads them, plays them on stick figures, writes one clip back and leaves the rest of the bank untouched[/*]
[*][b]movemgr.bin[/b] - the MOVE graph, which animation plays when. Read as rules and written back one situation at a time[/*]
[*][b].ai.rml[/b] - AI brains. Unpacked to editable text and compiled back, merged plan by plan across mods[/*]
[*][b].rtx[/b] - RealTree, the procedural trees. Decoded into branches and leaves, drawn in the map and model viewers[/*]
[*][b].fc2model[/b] - JackAll's own model pack: JSON and PNG, no Dunia bytes. Written and read. The only thing the Blender add-on ever sees[/*]
[*][b].sbao[/b] - streamed audio: music and dialogue. Splits into header and ogg, plays it, exports ogg or mp3, imports anything ffmpeg reads[/*]
[*][b].spk[/b] - sound banks. Decodes to XML and back, byte for byte on every retail bank, edits a bank as a tree, extracts and imports ogg vorbis and IMA ADPCM, and labels every retail bank with what it is[/*]
[*][b].mft[/b] - fonts. Decoded, encoded, and drawn from any TrueType or OpenType file[/*]
[*][b].hkx[/b] - Havok collision. Reads and writes every shipped file, editor tab with a 3D view, decodes to XML and back, rebuilds the MOPP tree after an edit[/*]
[*][b].sdat[/b] - terrain per sector: heights, surface types, water, baked light. It's what the map viewport draws, plus a grayscale height preview per file. A new world's terrain plan is written into it[/*]
[*][b].nvm[/b] - AI navmesh: the walkable triangles, their corners, the links between them and the cover points. Read, drawn on the map, and built with Recast from the terrain and every object's collision[/*]
[*][b].sav[/b] - savegames. Reads the header, screenshot and DLC list, edits and writes the PersistenceDB[/*]
[*][b]depload.dat[/b] - dependency list per asset. Parsed. Feeds the reference panel and has its own view[/*]
[*][b]Domino .lua (+ .debug.lua)[/b] - generated mission scripts. Parsed back into a node graph, with pin names and the original editor names[/*]
[*][b].png[/b] - not a game format. Own encoder and decoder, used for model pack textures[/*]
[*][b].xml, .lua, .desc[/b] - plain text files. Highlighted view, with a diff against the original when modded[/*]
[/list]

These are only recognized, not decoded. You can still browse, export and replace them, they just stay raw bytes: .bik (video), .feu (old UI), .wem, shader binaries, .srl and .zsr (streaming data), .luab and .luac (compiled Lua), .loc, .material.bin, .sctr, .tree, .cbatch, .terrainnode.bdl and .dpax.

[line]

[size=5][b]Where it's going[/b][/size]

JackAll started as a mod installer, and that part has moved out of the way. The install pipeline is now a [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/2143]Vortex extension[/url], so FC2 mods install from Nexus like they do for any other game. The extension is only a front end, not a second tool: Vortex does the downloading, staging, enabling and load order, and every step that needs the real game archives calls JackAll's command line. That way the mod rules exist in one place and can't drift apart. If you only want to install mods, use Vortex. JackAll is what you want when you're making one.

The rest grew way past "file manager". There's a real 3D map editor over the actual worlds and over new worlds of your own, a real model exporter with its own file format and a Blender add-on on the other side of it, editors for the AI and the animation rules, a visual reader for Domino missions, an archetype browser that resolves the override chains, and a savegame editor. A few of those don't exist anywhere else for this game, in any form. Domino is the one part that's still only a viewer.

None of this is really specific to Far Cry 2. Dunia is Ubisoft's engine across a decade of games and the formats are close relatives: an .fcb is an .fcb, an archive is an archive, a mesh is more or less the same mesh. Nothing stops someone from teaching JackAll Far Cry 3, 4, 5, 6 or whatever number they're on when you read this. That's more work than one person can do, which is exactly why the source is public.

[line]

[size=5][b]How to use[/b][/size]

[b]First run.[/b] Point JackAll at the folder that has bin\FarCry2.exe in it. It checks your archives against the hashes of a clean 1.03 install and tells you if something is already different. It still works in that case, but then "original" means whatever you gave it. The tool writes three things next to the exe: config.ini (your game folder and mod list, you can edit it by hand), data\ (dictionaries and caches, don't touch it), and workspace\.

[b]The workspace is where your edits live.[/b] Everything you change inside JackAll, a texture, an entity, an applied model, gets written into workspace\ as a normal file on its real game path. It's always last in the mod list, so your own work wins over every mod under it, and you can switch it off with the checkbox like any other mod when you want to see the game without it. There is no export or package step: [b]the workspace is the mod[/b]. Zip what's inside it and that zip is a mod anyone can drop into JackAll or install through Vortex.

[b]Edits are saved per entity, not per file.[/b] This is where JackAll works differently from the old Gibbed way. With Gibbed's tools you convert a whole .fcb to XML, edit it, and ship the whole file back, which means two mods that both touch the entity library always conflict, even when one changed a rifle and the other changed a jeep. JackAll splits the file into one piece per entity and saves only that piece, on a path like entitylibrary.fcb\vehicle\land\jeep.xml. Two mods on different entities never meet. Two mods on different fields of the same entity get merged against the original version. Only a real conflict, same field with two different values, needs you, and then JackAll shows it as a conflict instead of quietly picking one.

[b]Domino is read only.[/b] The Domino viewer rebuilds a mission graph so you can read it, and there's no way back to the Lua. It's still worth having for understanding the game, and it says viewer on purpose, so nobody spends an evening editing and then loses it.

[b]One thing worth repeating.[/b] A savegame keeps values that overlap almost every entity library, and an .fcb mod cannot override what the save already stored. If your data mod looks like it does nothing, start a new game before you decide the mod is broken.

[line]

[size=5][b]CLI[/b][/size]

jackall-cli.exe is the same thing without a window. It's a separate file, most of the format commands don't even need the game installed, and every mod command takes --json: one JSON object on stdout, progress on stderr, so your script never has to read text meant for humans.

[size=4][b]Mod commands[/b][/size]

The same code the Mods tab runs.

[list]
[*][b]mod status --game <dir>[/b] - tells you if a folder is a Far Cry 2 install and what state its patch archive is in[/*]
[*][b]mod inspect <folder or zip> --game <dir>[/b] - tells you if an archive is a normal mod layer or an old full patch mod, and where its file tree starts[/*]
[*][b]mod import-legacy --game <dir> --from <zip> --out <dir>[/b] - turns an old style full patch.dat/patch.fat mod into a normal layer folder[/*]
[*][b]mod lint --game <dir> --layer <dir>[/b] - lists archetype edits that a later entity library overrides, so they do nothing in game[/*]
[*][b]mod build --game <dir> --layer <a> --layer <b> ...[/b] - builds the clean patch plus your layers into the game's archive pair. Order matters, later layers win[/*]
[*][b]mod restore --game <dir>[/b] - puts the clean patch.dat/patch.fat back and undoes every build[/*]
[/list]

[code]jackall-cli mod build --game "C:\Games\Far Cry 2" --layer mods\bettersights --layer workspace[/code]

[size=4][b]Format commands[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]legacy analyze / changes / check / pick[/b] - lists every change an old full patch mod makes, groups them into features, and builds a layer with only the features you pick[/*]
[*][b]archive extract <file.fat>[/b] - unpacks and decompresses an archive. --names turns hashes back into real paths, --filter narrows it down[/*]
[*][b]fcb decode / fcb encode[/b] - an .fcb object tree to XML and back[/*]
[*][b]rml decode / rml encode[/b] - a binary .rml to plain XML and back[/*]
[*][b]mgb decode / mgb encode / mgb verify[/b] - a Magma UI package to editable XML and back. verify checks it only uses names it declares[/*]
[*][b]xbt extract / xbt build[/b] - splits an .xbt into .dds plus header XML, and puts it back together. build also takes a bare .dds[/*]
[*][b]hkx decode / encode / tree / move / turn / resize / add-box / remove / refresh / check[/b] - collision to XML and back, shapes edited by number, and a check for what would break in game[/*]
[*][b]xbg export <mesh.xbg>[/b] - converts a model's geometry to a Wavefront .obj[/*]
[*][b]fc2model export / extract / inspect[/b] - builds a model pack, writes a changed pack back out as game files laid out as a mod layer, or lists what's in a pack[/*]
[*][b]sbao extract / sbao build[/b] - splits an .sbao into .ogg plus header, and puts it back together[/*]
[*][b]spk list / extract / import / decode / encode / label[/b] - lists a sound bank, pulls one record out as .ogg or .wav, replaces one, turns a bank into XML and back, or labels every bank with what it is[/*]
[*][b]xbm show / new / set / lint[/b] - reads, makes and edits materials, and finds what the game would ignore[/*]
[*][b]mft build[/b] - draws a game font from a TrueType or OpenType file[/*]
[*][b]ai unpack / pack / fragments[/b] - a brain to editable text and back, or only the plans and tasks you changed as fragments[/*]
[*][b]move clone[/b] - gives a new weapon index every animation rule of an existing gun[/*]
[*][b]world new / world pack[/b] - starts a new world as real game files, and builds its sectors, entity library, terrain, vegetation and navmesh[/*]
[*][b]navmesh build[/b] - the AI's navmesh from the terrain and every placed object's collision[/*]
[*][b]xref build / xref to / xref from[/b] - indexes every hash reference in the game, then answers what points at a file and what a file points at[/*]
[/list]

[code]jackall-cli fc2model export graphics/weapons/primary/ak47/ak47.xbg -g "C:\Games\Far Cry 2" --clips
jackall-cli xref to "graphics\_common\weapons\ak47\ak47_d.xbt" --game "C:\Games\Far Cry 2"[/code]

[size=4][b]Who it's for[/b][/size]

The CLI is for people who want Far Cry 2 in their own toolchain: a build script that rebuilds your mod from source assets, a CI job that checks a model still round trips, a batch run over a few thousand textures, or a completely different mod manager. The JSON is the stable part. stdout is the result, stderr is progress, and a failure is one JSON object with ok false and an error message, exit code 1, never a random line on the wrong stream.

Vortex is the proof that it works. It drives jackall-mi.exe, a smaller build with only the four commands an installer really needs, and its JSON is identical to jackall-cli's mod commands. So the extension can use either one, and neither of them is a rewrite of the other.

[line]

[size=5][b]A word on intellectual property[/b][/size]

JackAll's .fcb, .rml and .fat decoding stands on ten years of community reverse engineering: Gibbed's original Dunia tools, wobatt's better hash decoding, and every forum post and Discord thread that ever pasted a working piece of format knowledge into the void.

So, to everyone who ever uploaded code, a hex dump, or a "here's how the header works" post that this tool's understanding of the format goes back to: thank you for your service.
